<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Radama II</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1829–1863</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Radama II was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madagascar">Madagascar</a> from 1861 to 1863, the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ranavalona-i">Ranavalona I</a>. He reversed his mother's policies, freeing Christians, opening the kingdom to foreigners and missionaries and granting vast concessions to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-francois-lambert">Joseph-François Lambert</a>. His favourites and policies alienated the nobility, and he was strangled in a coup in May 1863, after which his widow <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rasoherina">Rasoherina</a> reigned under the prime minister.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/radama-ii.jpg" alt="Radama II" width="320" height="387" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: William Ellis · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Radama_II_with_crown.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
